\begin{abstract}
Safety evaluations of obfuscated requests must distinguish failure to recover the
request from unsafe behavior after its recovery. We study this distinction with
PolyJigsaw, which interleaves fragments of parallel translations and requests an
English reconstruction followed by an answer. Reconstruction-gated attack success
rate (ASR) requires judged semantic equivalence and a primary answer label of
\emph{Unsafe} or \emph{Controversial}; it does not establish harmful usefulness.
We report the gate and answer-label components separately. On 1,727 held-out Lingua-SafetyBench items against
Qwen2.5-7B, the primary-judge joint rate is 0.693 for the development-selected
interleaving condition, compared with 0.253 for direct English and 0.585 for Finnish
translation. Comparisons with span-based code-switching use smaller, explicitly
matched subsets. The corresponding interleaving estimate is 0.631 under a second
safety judge, while the translation estimate changes to 0.281. English-only framing
controls and reconstruction-sensitive scoring show why non-refusal alone is
insufficient evidence of a faithful safety failure. Results vary across target
models, language loads, and reasoning modes; no configuration is established as
universally best. An output-filter analysis reduces one observed gated rate from
0.700 to 0.079, while input-detector results depend on the statistic and comparison
population. These findings support evaluating reconstructed meaning alongside final
answers. They do not identify an internal safety mechanism, validate a universal
capability law, or substitute automated judges for human assessment.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Safety failures on transformed requests pose an evaluation problem: an unsafe
completion may follow a faithful interpretation, a corrupted interpretation, or an
unrelated response. A binary non-refusal score does not distinguish these cases
\citep{souly2024strongreject,mazeika2024harmbench}. This distinction matters when the
input combines several languages and requires explicit reconstruction.

We study PolyJigsaw, a text-only construction that interleaves fragments of released
parallel translations. The target is asked to emit a reconstructed English request
and a separate answer. Our central question is whether observed unsafe behavior
co-occurs with recovery of the original meaning, and how this relation changes with
input structure, target model, and evaluator.



Parallel-language interleaving differs from submitting a single translation or a
code-switched sentence \citep{yong2023lowresource,deng2024multilingual,yoo2024csrt}.
However, the presence of several languages does not itself prove that all of them
are used in reasoning. English is present in every main condition, and ordered
interleaving preserves the order of its fragments. Selecting and joining English
fragments is therefore a possible solution. We measure the resulting reconstruction
rather than assuming a particular internal computation.

Our contribution is an empirical comparison of how interpretation changes with
the scoring convention and evaluator in this setting, organized around three points:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Joint evaluation.} We report the reconstruction gate, answer labels,
and their same-response conjunction. Their mathematical combination is not a new
metric; the empirical question is which reported failures survive both checks.
Nonpassing gates include missing or malformed outputs as well as semantic errors.
\item \textbf{Controlled comparisons and evaluator sensitivity.} We compare
released translations, code-switching, reconstruction framing, and structural
variants, distinguish full-coverage from matched-subset results, and quantify changes
under alternative reconstruction and safety judges.
\item \textbf{Boundaries and defensive implications.} Cross-target, reasoning-mode,
and defense studies expose cases where the pattern weakens or reverses. Exploratory
capability fits are kept separate from confirmed results; we do not claim validated
online adaptation or query-efficient optimization.
\end{itemize}

\section{Related Work}
\paragraph{Obfuscation and multilingual safety.}
Prior work evaluates harmful requests through low-resource translation
\citep{yong2023lowresource,deng2024multilingual,wang2024alllanguages}, code-switching
\citep{yoo2024csrt}, ciphers \citep{yuan2024cipherchat}, code transformations
\citep{lv2024codechameleon}, ASCII art \citep{jiang2024artprompt}, and payload
splitting \citep{kang2024exploiting}. These studies motivate testing how a change in
representation interacts with safety behavior. PolyJigsaw uses parallel-language
fragments and an explicit reconstruction output. Its measured distinction is the
joint reconstruction-and-unsafe-answer event, rather than evidence that every
fragment is benign or that multilingual reasoning is necessary.

\paragraph{Search and reasoning interventions.}
Gradient-based and iterative methods explore prompts using different access and
query assumptions \citep{zou2023universal,chao2023pair,liu2024autodan,yu2023gptfuzzer,mehrotra2024tap}.
Decomposition and flipping methods also vary their transformation and instruction
procedures \citep{li2024drattack,liu2024flipattack,ding2024renellm}. Our selected
baseline implementations do not reproduce every component of those procedures.
MIDAS \citep{midas2026} studies dispersion and reconstruction through multiple
images, while cognitive-overload and reasoning-extension studies motivate examining
inference demands \citep{xu2024cognitive,kuo2025hcot}. Their proposed mechanisms
should not be assumed to hold for the text-only experiments here.

\paragraph{Evaluation and defense.}
HarmBench and StrongREJECT emphasize that non-refusal alone is an inadequate success
criterion \citep{mazeika2024harmbench,souly2024strongreject}. Our gate adds an
explicit meaning-recovery requirement, but the binary unsafe-answer label does not
fully measure harmful usefulness or original-request fulfillment. Safety judges
including Qwen3Guard and MD-Judge provide scalable measurements
\citep{qwen3guard2025,li2024salad}; their agreement is distinct from validity against
human labels. Input reminders \citep{xie2023selfreminder}, paraphrasing and
perplexity checks \citep{jain2023baseline,alon2023detecting}, and output moderation
address different stages. We report their evaluated behavior and sample limitations
without treating one failed intervention as evidence against all defenses at that
stage.

\section{Method}
\subsection{Threat model and formulation}
We assume a black-box, single-shot adversary (no gradients, no iterative
refinement) who can only query the target. Let $q$ be a harmful English request
with official parallel translations $\{q^{(L)}\}_{L\in\mathcal{L}}$. PolyJigsaw
constructs an interleaved prompt
\[
x^{*} = \mathrm{Interleave}\big(\{\phi_L(q^{(L)})\}_{L\in\mathcal{L}}\big),
\quad \text{s.t.}\quad \mathrm{Reconstruct}\circ\mathrm{Deinterleave}(x^{*})\approx q,
\]
where $\phi_L$ splits sentence $q^{(L)}$ into fragments and assigns fragment IDs.
Each fragment is a verbatim substring of an official translation; \emph{the main construction reuses the released
translations}; a separate experiment changes the translation source. The number of languages
$n=|\mathcal{L}|$ is the dispersion load. Figure~\ref{fig:method} depicts the full pipeline and the two-judge gated
evaluation; Algorithm~\ref{alg:polyjigsaw} states it precisely.


\paragraph{Fragment construction.} Released parallel sentences are divided into
contiguous token runs, or character runs for Chinese and Japanese, and assigned
display identifiers after interleaving. The main experiment scores two output
sections, \texttt{[RECONSTRUCTED]} and \texttt{[ANSWER]}. Earlier diagnostic probes
also requested language-wise fragment-ID assignments, but those assignments are not
a requirement of the main success metric. Fragment provenance does not imply that
individual fragments are innocuous or that the complete input conceals its intent.

