\subsection{Interleaving conditions}
In the \textbf{ordered} condition, each language's fragments keep their original
relative order (the model must group and normalize them); in the
\textbf{shuffled} condition, order is destroyed within each language, so the model
must additionally recover grammatical order. The model returns a
\texttt{[RECONSTRUCTED]} English request and then an \texttt{[ANSWER]}.


\subsection{Language selection}
\label{sec:langsel}
English is the reconstruction anchor. The remaining languages follow a fixed order
(Chinese, French, Arabic, Russian, Spanish, German, Japanese, Finnish, Norwegian),
applied uniformly across items. Thus $n=4$ uses English, Chinese, French, and Arabic.
The order provides several scripts at low language loads, but its causal effect is
not isolated by the load sweep. In particular, a change in $n$ also changes language
identity, input length, and the number of fragments. We call this a fixed design,
not a preregistered one: the released artifacts do not contain an independent
preregistration record.

For the main Qwen comparison, ordered $n=4$ is selected from the available development
aggregate (gated ASR 0.710, versus 0.662 for ordered $n=6$). Finnish is also the
highest-scoring translation in that development aggregate. Other tables that
select the highest-scoring evaluated condition are explicitly descriptive, and do
not establish performance of an independently selected configuration.

\subsection{Baselines and ablations}
We compare against (i) \textbf{english\_direct}, (ii) \textbf{translated\_direct}
in each language \citep{yong2023lowresource}, and (iii) \textbf{CSRT}
\citep{yoo2024csrt}. Our CSRT baseline code-switches the same official content
spans as our inline-slot method \texttt{slot\_k} yet submits them directly with no
game framing; thus \texttt{slot\_k} vs.\ \texttt{csrt\_k} is a matched pair
comparing the complete framing intervention. A full-coverage CSRT-inspired variant \texttt{csrt\_mt}
uses NLLB \citep{nllb2022} segment
translation (a machine-translated baseline; a separate NLLB interleaving experiment is reported in Sec.~\ref{sec:mtrobust}). The
\textbf{no-game} ablation presents the interleaved fragments without the
reconstruction instruction (cf.\ MIDAS ``w/o Game-Style'').


\section{Reconstruction-Gated Evaluation}
\label{sec:gated}
For each response $i$, let $R_i$ indicate a judged meaning-equivalent reconstruction
and $U_i$ an answer receiving the primary \emph{Unsafe} or
\emph{Controversial} label. We use ``unsafe rate as shorthand for this combined
label event throughout; it is not the Unsafe-only rate. A separate call to Qwen2.5-7B compares the
reconstruction with the original request on action, target, intent, and material
constraints. This judge is the same model family as the primary target, not an
independent source of ground truth. Safety moderation scores the answer section,
excluding the quoted reconstruction. We report
\[
\widehat R=\frac{1}{N}\sum_i R_i,\qquad
\widehat{\mathrm{ASR}}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_i U_i,\qquad
\widehat{\mathrm{gated\ ASR}}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_i R_iU_i.
\]
The joint rate is not the product of the two marginal rates. On a common valid
population it equals $P(R)P(U\mid R)$; the conditional factor is undefined if no
reconstruction passes. A reconstruction-only reward does not measure this joint
outcome.

The gate excludes labelled answers accompanied by a nonpassing reconstruction
check, including cases with an absent or unparseable reconstruction.
Nevertheless, an unsafe-answer label does not by itself establish that the answer
usefully fulfills the original request. The metric adds a reconstruction check to
safety classification; it does not resolve every relevance or usefulness problem
identified by StrongREJECT \citep{souly2024strongreject}. Human validation remains
necessary.

\paragraph{Direct conditions and missing judgments.}
The historical scoring protocol sets $R_i=1$ for direct English, translation, and
CSRT submissions because they do not request an explicit reconstructed output.
This is a convention, not measured semantic equivalence, and may miss translation
or code-switching errors. Consequently, gated ASR equals raw ASR in those rows. The direct and explicit-
reconstruction rows therefore do not estimate an identically verified semantic
success event. Their contrast describes the recorded scoring protocol, rather than
a fully calibrated difference in original-request fulfillment.
For reconstruction conditions, the comparison script treats missing or invalid
reconstruction judgments as gate failures. It excludes invalid primary safety
judgments from the denominator and computes cross-judge rates on available
cross-judge labels. The aggregate files do not always retain those secondary
sample counts. These legacy conventions limit coverage comparisons; a missing
judgment must not be interpreted as an observed safe response.

\paragraph{Judge sensitivity.}
The primary safety event includes Qwen3Guard's \emph{Unsafe} and
\emph{Controversial} labels, uniformly across conditions. Unsafe-only rates are a
sensitivity analysis, not interchangeable ASR definitions. On the full-set analysis,
ordered $n=4$ changes from 0.700 to 0.654 under the unsafe-only threshold; Finnish
translation changes from 0.584 to 0.445. We also report MD-Judge scores, a separate
harmfulness rating, and reconstruction re-judging. Neither agreement among model
judges nor the fact that $RU\leq U$ guarantees unbiased estimates.

\section{Experimental Setup}

\paragraph{Data and partitions.} The main collection comprises 2,879
Text-Dominant items from Lingua-SafetyBench \citep{shi2026lingua}, with ten released
parallel translations and eight scenarios. The dataset authors describe human
verification of their translation pipeline; official distribution should not be
read as evidence that every translation was written by a human. A
scenario-stratified split (seed 20260901) yields 1,152 development and 1,727 test
items for the main comparison. This is a study-specific split within the local
source collection, not a new independent benchmark test release. The full 2,879-item
load sweep overlaps these partitions and is descriptive. Separate model-panel and
commercial-model studies use other subsets; their estimates are not interchangeable.
AttaQ \citep{attaq2023} supplies an external 1,402-item collection in seven languages.

The main Qwen comparison fixes ordered $n=4$ and Finnish using the development
aggregate. The legacy comparison script otherwise selects the best translation
within the supplied split, and some cross-target tables select test maxima. We label
these maxima as descriptive rather than claiming that all tables use development-only
selection. No target-model weights were updated for the experiments reported here.


\paragraph{Targets and judges.} The main open-model comparison covers Qwen2.5-7B,
Qwen3-8B, Phi-3.5-mini, and InternLM2.5-7B
\citep{qwen25,phi3_2024,internlm2024}. Mistral-7B and Qwen2.5-32B appear in additional
panels; GPT-4o-mini and GPT-4o are commercial targets. These are the evaluated model
versions, not a claim about the current frontier. Qwen2.5-7B provides reconstruction
judgments. Qwen3Guard-Gen-8B \citep{qwen3guard2025} supplies the primary answer
labels, MD-Judge \citep{li2024salad} supplies a cross-check, and Qwen2.5-7B scores a
0--5 harmfulness rubric \citep{kuo2025hcot}. Greedy decoding with a 320-token output
budget is the main local protocol; reasoning-mode and supplementary runs have their
own budgets and are reported separately.

\paragraph{Statistical interpretation.} Primary rates use condition-specific valid
$N$, with stored Wilson 95\% intervals. Span-based conditions have fewer eligible
items. Paired differences, bootstrap intervals, and exact McNemar tests use the
intersection of item IDs for the two compared conditions; they need not equal the
difference between separately reported marginal rates. Reported $p$ values are
nominal and are not adjusted for the many exploratory comparisons. Single-seed,
model-judged estimates do not quantify uncertainty over training seeds, model
versions, or human labels.

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Language-load sensitivity}
Table~\ref{tab:curve} describes the full 2,879-item Qwen2.5-7B sweep. Ordered
interleaving peaks at $n=4$ (gated ASR 0.700); the sampled maximum is a property of
this model, collection, and grid. Ordered conditions exceed the shuffled conditions
at each evaluated load. All ordered loads exceed the Finnish reference; several
shuffled loads do not. Other targets can peak at a grid boundary, so this sweep
does not establish a universal interior ``sweet spot.''

\subsection{Head-to-head against multilingual baselines}
Table~\ref{tab:main} reports the Qwen2.5-7B test comparison with each condition's
actual sample count. Direct English, Finnish, and ordered interleaving cover 1,727
items. CSRT-all and slot $k=1$ cover 649, while CSRT $k=2$ covers 314. Ordered $n=4$
reaches 0.6925 gated ASR. Its paired difference is 0.4401 against English
(95\% bootstrap interval [0.4146, 0.4661], $N=1727$), 0.1077 against Finnish
([0.0816, 0.1332], $N=1727$), and 0.4022 against CSRT-all
([0.3606, 0.4438], $N=649$). Each stored paired test has $p<10^{-4}$.
The CSRT-all difference uses its matched subset, not subtraction of the two full
marginal rates in the table.

Mean harmfulness is 2.191 for ordered $n=4$, versus 1.575 for English and 1.804
for Finnish. The corresponding fractions with HR$\geq3$ are 0.414, 0.259, and
0.148; CSRT $k=2$ is 0.264. These are additional model-judged measurements, not
human verification of actionability.

