\paragraph{Encoding and obfuscation comparisons.}
The tested Base64 variant has zero gated success on Qwen2.5-7B, and payload
splitting reaches 0.371 (Table~\ref{tab:encoding}). This describes the evaluated
prompt and decoding budget; it is not evidence that the target cannot decode any
Base64 input. Other supplementary experiments implement selected character-flipping,
word-flipping, and decomposition variants
\citep{liu2024flipattack,li2024drattack}. They do not reproduce every search procedure
or adaptive component of the published methods. We therefore make comparisons
between these recorded implementations, not claims of superiority over entire
attack families.

Dictionary non-refusal can differ sharply from reconstruction-gated scoring. On
Qwen, character flipping has dictionary ASR 0.870 and gated ASR 0.000, whereas word
flipping has dictionary ASR 0.933 and gated ASR 0.020. The discrepancy demonstrates
that non-refusal is insufficient to establish the joint event. It does not alone
establish human-rated uselessness of every response. Supplementary cross-model
comparisons and their protocol limitations are reported in Appendix~\ref{app:baselines}.


\subsection{Judge sensitivity across targets}
\label{sec:judgerobust}\label{sec:generalization}
On the full sweep of 34,548 responses, the two safety judges agree on 86.4\% of
labels (Cohen's $\kappa=0.71$). In the main test comparison, interleaving changes
from 0.693 to 0.631 across judges, while Finnish changes from 0.585 to 0.281.
The smaller interleaving difference is evidence of relative stability in these
measurements, not proof that either judge is accurate.

Table~\ref{tab:targets} reports descriptive, within-target maxima and their
cross-judge scores. Translation is higher than interleaving under the primary judge
on Phi-3.5 and InternLM. GPT-4o additionally judges 604 selected disagreement rows.
Its agreement on this selected set cannot be extrapolated to the complete target
population or establish an overall ranking between methods. We retain the
translation-favorable cases and do not define ``strong alignment'' from the same
results to explain away a method's failures.

\subsection{Commercial-model results}
\label{sec:transfer}
The original commercial comparison uses 200-item subsets for GPT-4o-mini and GPT-4o
(Tables~\ref{tab:frontier} and~\ref{tab:frontier2}). GPT-4o-mini has direct-English
gated ASR 0.12 and a reported interleaving maximum of 0.78. On GPT-4o, ordered
interleaving at $n=4$ is 0.195 versus 0.130 for English, with nominal paired
McNemar $p=0.019$. Finnish translation reaches 0.255 under the primary judge and
0.085 under MD-Judge; interleaving is 0.195 and 0.165 respectively. Thus the
method ranking is judge-dependent. The supplementary GPT-4o structural panel uses
250 items and different conditions; its larger estimates must not be substituted
for this 200-item result.


\subsection{Detectability and defenses}
\label{sec:defenses}
An external Qwen3Guard input classifier flags 90\% of direct-English prompts and
75.7\% of ordered $n=4$ interleavings (Table~\ref{tab:detect}). The larger pass rate
for interleaving is a result about this classifier; most interleaved prompts are
still flagged. It neither implies that fragments are individually benign nor
locates the target model's internal safety checks.

The evaluated Self-Reminder-style system instruction
\citep{xie2023selfreminder} changes ordered $n=4$ gated ASR from 0.693 to 0.692,
while the English and Finnish rates decrease (Table~\ref{tab:defense}). A
paraphrase-then-answer pipeline reduces the interleaving rate to 0.408. These are
specific intervention results, not general resistance to input defenses.

Perplexity offers a separate, limited comparison. Full-sequence perplexity
separates interleaved prompts from direct harmful English with AUC 0.016 in the
chosen high-perplexity direction. This is a reversal of direction, not absence of
separation: negating the score would give AUC 0.984 on the same sample. Windowed
maximum perplexity has AUC 0.98 on a different, smaller sample. Neither comparison
includes a representative benign population, so neither establishes a deployable
false-positive rate or a general benign-versus-harmful detector.

A retrospective output-filter analysis applies MD-Judge to generated answers and
reduces a full-set primary gated rate from 0.700 to 0.079, rejecting 88.7\% of
previously counted successes. MD-Judge differs from Qwen3Guard, but the evaluators
may share correlated errors. Benign utility, deployment latency, and an
independently labeled residual error rate were not established. The result motivates
output assessment alongside input checks rather than replacing all input defenses.
