\section{Discussion and Limitations}
\label{sec:discussion}
The main empirical finding is that the reported rate and sometimes the method
ranking change with reconstruction gating and evaluator choice. A high reconstruction rate alone does not imply a
safety failure, and a low rate caused by truncation does not demonstrate successful
refusal. These distinctions also apply when comparing different prompt structures
and model families.

\paragraph{Causal scope.} The experiments intervene on input construction and
instructions, not on internal safety modules. They cannot establish that safety
checks occur only at the input, that reconstruction suppresses a re-check, or that
cognitive overload is the mechanism. English anchoring permits a language-selection
shortcut; load, language identity, length, and fragmentation also covary. We report
associations and complete prompt-intervention effects within these limits.

\paragraph{Measurement and generalization.} Both reconstruction and safety labels
are model judgments, including a primary reconstruction judge from the same family
as a target. Cross-judge agreement, rubric scores, and disagreement adjudication do
not replace human validation. A labeling sheet exists, but completed independent
human judgments are not established by the available artifacts. Direct-condition
reconstruction is assumed, missing-label conventions differ by stage, and
secondary denominators are not always preserved in aggregates. Some reported
conditions are test-set maxima; their values are descriptive and can be optimistic.
No generalization to unseen configurations or online selection is established.

\paragraph{Defensive scope.} The output-filter result is a retrospective
cross-classifier analysis without a representative benign-utility evaluation.
Perplexity experiments compare transformed harmful inputs with direct harmful
inputs, not a deployment mixture. More complete defense conclusions require both
harmful-response error and benign utility to be assessed on independent data.

\section{Conclusion}
PolyJigsaw provides a setting for measuring safety behavior under cross-lingual
reconstruction. On the evaluated collections, reconstruction framing is associated
with higher joint reconstruction-and-unsafe-answer rates in several conditions,
with substantial dependence on target, configuration, and judge. Matched-subset
comparisons, explicit denominators, reconstruction-sensitive scoring, and negative
results are essential to interpreting these observations. The evidence motivates
assessment of reconstructed meaning and final answers alongside input checks;
it does not validate a universal difficulty rule or an internal causal mechanism.

